and now we are on to just sort of the  last topic which has to do with the  resampling approach and resampling  approaches are are really really  powerful tool  um  that gives us a way to conduct  hypothesis testing either a single  hypothesis test or a lot of hypothesis  tests  um in a way that's relatively assumption  free  so what is this all about well in this  lecture so far we've assumed that we're  in the scenario where we want to test  some null hypothesis H naught we have a  test statistic T and most importantly we  know or else we feel like we can assume  the distribution of t under the null  hypothesis  and that's how we were able to compute  the p-value was by knowing the  distribution of the test statistic under  the null hypothesis so remember this  figure from before and the distribution  of t under the null hypothesis was this  yellow curve in this in this particular  example the distribution of t under the  null was a um approximately a normal  distribution but in general depending on  your null hypothesis depending on your T  statistic that null distribution of the  test statistic could be a different  distribution an unknown or it could be  unknown right and in fact that's the  idea here is what if this theoretical  null distribution is actually unknown  we just don't know what it looks like or  alternatively we could make some  assumptions under which we might know  what it looks like but maybe we we feel  a little bit weird making those  assumptions because we don't really  think they're going to hold  oh reward they might not hold at least  we're worried they might not hold we  just want to do something that's a  little bit more assumption free  so one way we can do this is using a  resampling type of approach and the  thing we should say about resampling  approaches before we get into the  details is that it's a very general  framework that needs to be instantiated  in a specific way depending on the type  of hypothesis you're testing and the  type of test it is you're using So today  we're going to talk about what a  resampling approach will look like for  conducting a two-sample t-test but in  general if you're testing a different  type of null hypothesis it's not just a  difference in means or if you have a  test statistic that's not just a  two-sample T Test you're going to need  to figure out whether there is an  appropriate resampling approach for you  and also what that resampling approach  looks like so this is going to really  require some hard thinking and it's not  Plug and Play the way that a lot of  things that we've seen so far in this  lecture are so the idea is suppose that  we want to test the null hypothesis that  uh the mean in in one group equals the  mean in another group versus the  alternative that they're unequal and  here we have N Sub X independent  observations from X and N Sub y  independent observations from y well  then as we've already seen today the two  sample T statistic takes the form of  just the sample mean in group x minus  sample mean in group y divided by an  estimate of the standard deviation  between mu X of mu x minus mu y  and if n x and n y are large then as  we've already said t approximately  follows a normal 0 1 distribution under  the null hypothesis  but what happens if n x and n y are  small well in that case we actually  don't know the theoretical nil  distribution of T unless we make further  assumptions about the distribution of  the the data the distribution of the x's  and the Y's  so what we can do is we can try to take  a resampling approach and the idea  behind a resampling approach is that  instead of just like looking up in a  textbook with the theoretical null  distribution of T is or taking our word  for it or deriving it yourself if that's  your thing we're actually just going to  let a computer sort of simulate for us  what the null distribution of T would  look like under the null hypothesis
 

